            <ArticleTitle>Transcriptional Profiling of Cutaneous MRGPRD Free Nerve Endings and C-LTMRs.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2211-1247(15)00047-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.celrep.2015.01.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cutaneous C-unmyelinated MRGPRD(+) free nerve endings and C-LTMRs innervating hair follicles convey two opposite aspects of touch sensation: a sensation of pain and a sensation of pleasant touch. The molecular mechanisms underlying these diametrically opposite functions are unknown. Here, we used a mouse model that genetically marks C-LTMRs and MRGPRD(+) neurons in combination with fluorescent cell surface labeling, flow cytometry, and RNA deep-sequencing technology (RNA-seq). Cluster analysis of RNA-seq profiles of the purified neuronal subsets revealed 486 and 549 genes differentially expressed in MRGPRD-expressing neurons and C-LTMRs, respectively. We validated 48 MRGPD- and 68 C-LTMRs-enriched genes using a triple-staining approach, and the Cav3.3 channel, found to be exclusively expressed in C-LTMRs, was validated using electrophysiology. Our study greatly expands the molecular characterization of C-LTMRs and suggests that this particular population of neurons shares some molecular features with Aβ and Aδ low-threshold mechanoreceptors.</AbstractText>
